  "winner_texts": {
    "13": "I would recommend staying silent and respecting the performer's effort. Here's why:\n\nFirstly, the performer is likely already feeling anxious, nervous, and self-conscious about their\nperformance. Booing or expressing disapproval would only add to their discomfort and stress, which\nmight lead them to become defensive, emotional, or even worse, freeze up and lose focus.\n\nSecondly, criticizing someone's artistry or talent in public can be perceived as hurtful or unkind.\nAs a spectator, you're not an expert, and your opinion may not be constructive or helpful to the\nperformer. You might be coming from a place of disappointment or expectation, but others may still\nappreciate the performer's effort.\n\nThirdly, staying silent allows the performer to have their moment and receive an audience's\nfeedback, if any. Many performers value constructive criticism and are likely aware that they can't\nplease everyone all the time. If you truly care about the performer's improvement, you could\nconsider sharing your concerns privately with them after the show or providing a more specific,\nhelpful critique in a review or online post.\n\nLastly, sometimes, even with practice and preparation, performers have off days or may not be at\ntheir best. It's essential to acknowledge that they're making an effort and putting themselves out\nthere for the audience to enjoy.\n\nIf you must express your disapproval, consider it privately, constructively, and respectfully.\nHowever, in a public setting where you might influence others to boo, it's usually more respectful\nto keep silent, enjoy or appreciate what can be appreciated, or simply leave without expressing any\nnegative opinion."
